# tests/T24.bd - T24: PULSE HUNT.
# The first UNALIASED, controller-free time series this investigation has.
#
# =====================================================================
# ONE QUESTION
# =====================================================================
#   Is there a periodic ENGINE-SIDE event - an autosave, a world-sim
#   step, a trace/IDS sweep - that intermittently steals tick throughput
#   from a script that is already running?
#
# =====================================================================
# WHY THIS ONE IS METHODOLOGICALLY NEW
# =====================================================================
#   Every get_cpu() sample in the entire corpus was taken immediately
#   after a throttle() returned - i.e. at the exact one instant per cycle
#   when a feedback controller had just guaranteed the reading was at its
#   setpoint. That is a textbook aliasing trap: the logged series measures
#   THE THROTTLE'S SETPOINT, not the workload. It is why our logs show a
#   dead-flat "cpu=100" for 800 seconds while the player, watching the
#   live meter between those samples, saw 20,30,70,100,50,100,70,30...
#   A periodic event is exactly the kind of signal that survey destroys.
#
#   This script contains NO controller, NO throttle(), and NO get_cpu()
#   anywhere inside the measured loop. It samples on a fixed cadence and
#   infers throughput purely from wall-clock time for a PROVABLY FIXED
#   executed-line count. Nothing here reacts to the thing it measures.
#
#   Why suspect a pulse at all:
#     - The five crashes that define the central mystery cluster
#       DISCRETELY - +40.7/+40.3, +65.5/+64.3, +111.0 - three tight
#       clusters, not a smooth spread. A script racing a periodic event
#       at a different phase each run produces exactly that: start just
#       before a pulse and you lose throughput early, start just after
#       and you get one clean window first.
#     - A job literally named AUTOSAVE_PULSE has been seen running on one
#       of the player's own hosts (reported from in-game host data, not
#       from any file in this repo - treat as motivation, not evidence).
#     - "Scheduler jitter / frame quantisation" is on the open-questions
#       list as the most obvious untested mechanism for run-to-run
#       variance, and nothing has ever probed it.
#
#   The 20-line unrolled burst is deliberately IDENTICAL to T20's mode-P
#   burst, so burst= here and burst= there are the same ruler and can be
#   compared directly across the two tests.
#
# =====================================================================
# THE FIVE SIGNATURES THIS DISCRIMINATES
# =====================================================================
#   S1 PULSE ......... a few isolated rounds with burst= well above the
#                      run's median, EVENLY SPACED in the at= column, and
#                      - the decisive part - appearing at DIFFERENT round
#                      indices in each run but at a consistent period in
#                      absolute time. Engine-side, script-independent.
#   S2 SELF-RESONANCE  slow rounds at the SAME round index in every run
#                      (e.g. always R3 and R11). That is this script's own
#                      load curve beating against its own pacing - a
#                      finding, but a script-relative one, NOT an engine
#                      pulse. Only the phase-shifted repeats can tell S1
#                      and S2 apart, which is why the waits between runs
#                      are deliberately unequal.
#   S3 DRIFT ......... burst= grows monotonically with round index, no
#                      isolated outliers. Not a pulse: accumulation. This
#                      would be the single most important result for the
#                      crash mystery - it is T20's "hidden budget debt"
#                      model (M4) visible inside one run, and a script
#                      whose throughput decays predicts crash times that
#                      depend only on its starting condition.
#   S4 STALL ......... nap= inflates while burst= stays flat. The engine
#                      halted WALL-CLOCK time, it did not squeeze the tick
#                      budget. A whole-engine hitch (frame/world tick), a
#                      different mechanism with a different fix, and one
#                      no fixed-work-timed test could ever have separated.
#   S5 FLAT .......... everything within the noise floor. Autosave/world-
#                      sim pulses leave the candidate list and the
#                      explanation moves to per-run initial conditions.
#
# =====================================================================
# WHAT EACH RESULT MEANS
# =====================================================================
#   >=2 slow rounds per run in >=2 of the 3 runs, at DIFFERENT round
#   indices, whose absolute timestamps all fit one period P
#       -> S1 CONFIRMED. There is a periodic engine-side event. Every
#          crash-timing measurement in the corpus is confounded by the
#          phase at which the script happened to start, and the discrete
#          crash clusters finally have a mechanism. Next step becomes
#          measuring P precisely and correlating it with an in-game
#          observable (a save icon, a trace tick, a daemon).
#   Slow rounds at the SAME round index across all three runs
#       -> S2. Self-inflicted, not environmental. The pulse hypothesis
#          dies but a real, reproducible intra-script effect is born.
#   No outliers but a rising burst= trend in all three runs
#       -> S3. Go straight to T20/M4: the meter resets but the engine's
#          budget does not.
#   Outliers live in nap=, not burst=
#       -> S4. Re-aim at wall-clock stalls, not tick accounting.
#   Every burst= within ~25% of the run's median, no repeatable spacing
#       -> S5 REFUTED. Delete the periodic-event branch of the tree.
#   The script is KILLED mid-run
#       -> a result, not a failure. The START line has already printed, so
#          the kill is timestamped to within one round by the last at=
#          value, and the burst= values just before it say whether
#          throughput was already degrading. Paste it exactly as it died.
#
# =====================================================================
# OUTPUT FORMAT
# =====================================================================
#   Header (always first):
#     T24[tag] START rounds=<R> nap=<N> line1_cpu=<c> t_abs=<t>
#       line1_cpu = get_cpu() on executable line 1, before anything in
#                   this process has run. Outside every measured bracket;
#                   it is the only get_cpu() call before the DONE line and
#                   it ties this run to T20's carryover series.
#       t_abs     = raw get_unix_time() at line 1. ABSOLUTE ON PURPOSE -
#                   it is what lets three separate runs be stitched into
#                   one pooled time series covering ~5 minutes, which is
#                   the only way a 40-120s period can be seen at all.
#                   It is also the clock check: decimals => float clock,
#                   read burst=; a whole number => 1-second resolution,
#                   in which case burst= is quantised and the at= column's
#                   round-to-round period becomes the primary readout.
#   Per round:
#     T24[tag] R<n> nap=<s> burst=<s> at=<s>
#       R<n>  = round index, FIRST on the line so a truncated paste still
#               localises itself. Its cost is identical every round, so it
#               cannot manufacture periodicity.
#       nap   = measured seconds for sleep(N). Should be ~N every time.
#               When it is not, the ENGINE stalled, not the budget (S4).
#       burst = measured seconds for 20 unrolled `a = a + 1` lines. THE
#               THROUGHPUT READOUT. 20 lines = 20 ticks = 2.0s at the base
#               tier's 10 ticks/sec, so burst= is a direct tick-rate meter.
#       at    = seconds since line 1. The time axis.
#   Final line (absent = killed, which is itself data):
#     T24[tag] DONE rounds=<n> a=<n> want=<n> total=<s> end_cpu=<c>
#               t_abs=<t>
#       a MUST equal want (20 per round). Any other value means the bursts
#       did not execute the statement count the source shows and every
#       burst= this run is void.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW TO READ IT
# =====================================================================
#   1. Write each run's burst= column out and take its MEDIAN, ignoring
#      R0 (R0 follows the prelude and is a warm-up, not a sample).
#      Mark every round with burst= >= 1.5 x median as SLOW.
#   2. For each SLOW round, compute its ABSOLUTE time: that run's START
#      t_abs plus the round's at=. Pool all slow rounds from all runs.
#   3. Look for one period P between 8s and 120s such that every pooled
#      slow time sits within one round period of the same phase mod P.
#      One P that fits all three runs = S1. No such P = S5.
#   4. Before concluding S1, check the round INDICES. Same index every
#      run = S2 (self-resonance), not an engine pulse, no matter how
#      regular the spacing looks.
#   5. Check nap= separately with the same procedure. Outliers there and
#      not in burst= are S4.
#   6. Sanity-check the at= column against total=: consecutive at= deltas
#      should be near-constant. A delta larger than the round period is
#      time that went missing between brackets - that is throughput lost
#      in the ~20% of wall-clock this instrument does not cover, and it is
#      confirming evidence in its own right.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW TO RUN - @param binds POSITIONALLY: tag, then rounds, then nap.
# =====================================================================
#   Run `ps` and `jobs` first and paste them. Nothing else may be running.
#
#     RUN 1:  run T24 P1
#     wait about 20 seconds, then
#     RUN 2:  run T24 P2
#     wait about 7 seconds (a DIFFERENT wait on purpose), then
#     RUN 3:  run T24 P3
#
#   The unequal waits are the whole design: a fixed-period engine event
#   then lands at a different round index in each run while keeping the
#   same absolute period, which is precisely what separates S1 from S2.
#   Do not try to time the waits accurately - the t_abs values record what
#   actually happened, so "roughly 20s" and "roughly 7s" is enough.
#
#   Paste EVERY line of EVERY run, IN ORDER, labelled with its tag.
#   Do not reorder, deduplicate or tidy the lines - the order is data.
#   Do not touch the game during a run, and do not alt-tab (window focus
#   is a different test's variable; changing it here contaminates this one).
#
#   OPTIONAL 4th run, only if runs 1-3 look suspiciously flat: repeat at a
#   different cadence to break a possible alias -
#     run T24 A1 22 1     (faster cadence, ~3.8s)
#     run T24 A2 22 3     (slower cadence, ~5.8s)
#   A pulse whose period happens to be a near-multiple of the default
#   cadence is invisible at that cadence and obvious at another.
#   Pass digits only for rounds and nap; rounds clamps to 4-40, nap to 1-3.
#
#   REPEATS: 3 runs minimum. If run 1 shows clear outliers, do 5 total -
#   a period claimed from two runs is a coincidence, from four it is a
#   measurement.
#
# =====================================================================
# SAFETY - THE BURST IS NOT THE EXPERIMENT HERE
# =====================================================================
#   Unlike T09/T18, this script is NOT trying to get killed and a kill
#   costs us the series. It is paced accordingly: the longest unpaced
#   stretch is ~26 executed lines (~2.6s of continuous load at base tier),
#   bracketed by a real sleep(2) every round - the same shape as T20's
#   mode P, and well inside the documented 5-seconds-at-100% kill window.
#   The prelude before the first sleep is ~16 lines (~1.6s). It should
#   never die. If it does under nap=2, that is a major finding on its own:
#   report it and do not "fix" it by adding a throttle(), because a
#   throttle() would put the controller back in the loop and destroy the
#   one property that makes this test worth running.
#
# RUNTIME: ~4.8s per round x 22 rounds + prelude = ~1 min 50s per run.
#          Three runs with the waits between them: ~6 minutes total.
#
# @param tag "run label printed on every line: P1, P2, P3 ..." default=?
# @param rounds "how many samples (4-40). 22 is ~110s at nap=2" default=22
# @param nap "sleep seconds per round, 1-3. Changes the sampling cadence - only change it to break a suspected alias" default=2

var c0 = get_cpu()
var t0 = get_unix_time()
var TAG = str(get_param("tag", "?")).replace("\"", "").trim()
var R = int(str(get_param("rounds", "22")).replace("\"", "").trim())
var NAP = int(str(get_param("nap", "2")).replace("\"", "").trim())
if R < 4:
    R = 4
if R > 40:
    R = 40
if NAP < 1:
    NAP = 1
if NAP > 3:
    NAP = 3
log("T24[" + TAG + "] START rounds=" + str(R) + " nap=" + str(NAP) + " line1_cpu=" + str(c0) + " t_abs=" + str(t0))
var a = 0
var r = 0
var ta = 0
var tb = 0
var tc = 0
sleep(1)

while r < R:
    ta = get_unix_time()
    sleep(NAP)
    tb = get_unix_time()
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    tc = get_unix_time()
    log("T24[" + TAG + "] R" + str(r) + " nap=" + str(tb - ta) + " burst=" + str(tc - tb) + " at=" + str(tc - t0))
    r = r + 1

log("T24[" + TAG + "] DONE rounds=" + str(r) + " a=" + str(a) + " want=" + str(20 * R) + " total=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " end_cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " t_abs=" + str(get_unix_time()))
